{"url":"https://blog.back4app.com/firebase-pricing/","title":"Google Firebase Pricing Explained (2026)","description":"Please read the article Should ... databases. <strong>Google hosts your Firebase app free in the Spark plan, and in the Blaze plan, charges a modest fee per GB of application data stored and data transferred</strong>....","extra_snippets":["Please read the article Should You Choose Realtime DB or Cloud Firestore to know more about Firebase databases. Google hosts your Firebase app free in the Spark plan, and in the Blaze plan, charges a modest fee per GB of application data stored and data transferred.","Firebase pricing has two levels in 2026: the Spark plan (free, with fixed monthly limits on database, storage, and functions) and the Blaze plan (pay-as-you-go, which includes the free quotas and bills only for usage above them).","The Firebase real-time database provides free and unlimited read and write operations. To understand the full scope of services offered on Google Cloud, learn about other Google Cloud Database services.","Firebase provides 10,000 authentications a month, and beyond that, charges a different rate depending on your region. Google Cloud Functions, the Google Function as a Service product, is offered as part of the Firebase plans."]}

{"url":"https://firebase.google.com/docs/app-hosting/costs","title":"Understand App Hosting costs | Firebase App Hosting","description":"Since App Hosting requires that your project be on the Blaze pricing plan, we strongly recommend that you set up budget alerts and spend caps on Cloud Run. App Hosting relies on Cloud Run (see table above), so a spend cap on Cloud Run is essentially a spend cap on App Hosting, too. Your Firebase App Hosting project incurs costs once you exceed the pay-as-you-go Blaze pricing plan no-cost allowances.","extra_snippets":["[[[\"Easy to understand\",\"easyToUnderstand\",\"thumb-up\"],[\"Solved my problem\",\"solvedMyProblem\",\"thumb-up\"],[\"Other\",\"otherUp\",\"thumb-up\"]],[[\"Missing the information I need\",\"missingTheInformationINeed\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Too complicated / too many steps\",\"tooComplicatedTooManySteps\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Out of date\",\"outOfDate\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Samples / code issue\",\"samplesCodeIssue\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Other\",\"otherDown\",\"thumb-down\"]],[\"Last updated 2026-10-01 UTC.\"],[],[]]","When on this pricing plan, your project has the following no-cost limits for the Google Cloud products invoked by App Hosting: 1Cloud Storage is only used when deploying from local source with the Firebase CLI or by uploading a compressed archive in the Firebase console.","Since App Hosting requires that your project be on the Blaze pricing plan, we strongly recommend that you set up budget alerts and spend caps on Cloud Run. App Hosting relies on Cloud Run (see table above), so a spend cap on Cloud Run is essentially a spend cap on App Hosting, too. Your Firebase App Hosting project incurs costs once you exceed the pay-as-you-go Blaze pricing plan no-cost allowances.","Uncached Outgoing Bandwidth refers to the data transferred directly from the origin servers of the Firebase App Hosting service to fulfill user requests. Origin servers are located between an App Hosting backend's Cloud Run service and Cloud CDN. This occurs when the requested content isn't already stored in the Cloud CDN cache (that is, it's uncached)."]}

{"url":"https://www.budgetforge.dev/tools/firebase-pricing-2026","title":"Firebase Pricing in 2026: What You Actually Pay","description":"App Hosting, the managed full-stack option built on Cloud Run, <strong>bills compute plus bandwidth, with the first 10 GiB per month of bandwidth free as of August 1, 2025</strong>, per the 2026 pricing page.","extra_snippets":["App Hosting, the managed full-stack option built on Cloud Run, bills compute plus bandwidth, with the first 10 GiB per month of bandwidth free as of August 1, 2025, per the 2026 pricing page. It is a separate line from Hosting's older 10 GB storage and 360 MB/day transfer allowances, and the two are easy to conflate on a bill. Firebase Studio is scheduled to shut down on March 22, 2027. The AI app-building workspace launched in April 2025 and is being retired.","Firebase's Spark plan is free and stays free within daily quotas. As of 2026, Cloud Firestore on Spark gives you 50,000 document reads, 20,000 writes, and 20,000 deletes per day, plus 1 GiB stored and 10 GiB/month network egress. Hosting adds 10 GB of storage and 360 MB per day of transfer, and the Realtime Database 1 GB stored. Cloud Functions and Cloud Storage are not on Spark at all: both require Blaze, so the widely quoted 2 million invocations per month and 5 GB stored are Blaze no-cost allowances rather than free-plan quotas.","Yes. Firebase Studio, the AI app-building workspace launched in April 2025, is scheduled to shut down on March 22, 2027. It does not affect Firebase pricing: your Firestore, Cloud Functions, and Hosting bills are unchanged by Studio being retired.","It does not change the rate card, but it is the Firebase pricing question people keep asking in 2026, so to be clear: Studio going away does not touch your Firestore, Functions, or Hosting bill. ... Firebase has two plans. Spark is free and stays free within daily quotas. Blaze is pay-as-you-go: you keep the Spark allowances, unlock two products Spark cannot run at all, then pay per unit above the no-cost tier. Here is what each plan covers in 2026, pulled from Firebase's 2026 pricing page. ... Cloud Storage is the one line where Firebase quietly runs two rate cards at once, and the gap between them is large enough to change a plan."]}

{"url":"https://firebase.google.com/docs/hosting/usage-quotas-pricing","title":"Learn about usage levels, quotas, and pricing for Hosting | Firebase Hosting","description":"Your Hosting quota is project-level, not site-level or channel-level. You can upgrade your project to the Blaze billing plan to unlock additional paid storage and data transfer levels. Learn more about quotas and pricing for Firebase Hosting. We recommend setting up budget alerts for your project in the Google Cloud console.","extra_snippets":["[[[\"Easy to understand\",\"easyToUnderstand\",\"thumb-up\"],[\"Solved my problem\",\"solvedMyProblem\",\"thumb-up\"],[\"Other\",\"otherUp\",\"thumb-up\"]],[[\"Missing the information I need\",\"missingTheInformationINeed\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Too complicated / too many steps\",\"tooComplicatedTooManySteps\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Out of date\",\"outOfDate\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Samples / code issue\",\"samplesCodeIssue\",\"thumb-down\"],[\"Other\",\"otherDown\",\"thumb-down\"]],[\"Last updated 2026-10-01 UTC.\"],[],[]]","Your Hosting quota is project-level, not site-level or channel-level. You can upgrade your project to the Blaze billing plan to unlock additional paid storage and data transfer levels. Learn more about quotas and pricing for Firebase Hosting. We recommend setting up budget alerts for your project in the Google Cloud console.","For example, you might consider using budget notifications to programmatically disable Cloud Billing on a project. When you deploy new content to your site, you create a \"release\" that points to a specific version of content and configuration for your site. The files associated with each release (both new releases and any retained previous releases) are stored by Firebase.","This Hosting storage is independent and unrelated to any other storage for your Firebase project (like Cloud Storage for Firebase or database storage)."]}

